# 06 — Proposed CSS Architecture

Answers brief §4, §5, §15, §16, §17, §18.

---

## 1. The one hard constraint that shapes everything

Style Settings injects user overrides as:

```css
body.css-settings-manager             { --token: value; }   /* specificity (0,2,1) */
body.theme-light.css-settings-manager { --token: value; }   /* specificity (0,3,1) */
body.theme-dark.css-settings-manager  { --token: value; }   /* specificity (0,3,1) */
```

Therefore:

> ### 🔒 The Prime Prime Directive
>
> **Every themeable value is a CSS custom property declared at `body`, `.theme-dark`, or `.theme-light`, and consumed only via `var()` at the point of use.**
>
> - No `!important` on any themeable property.
> - No themeable value declared at a selector more specific than `.theme-dark`.
> - Selectors at the usage site may be arbitrarily specific — they only read variables.

This single rule guarantees that every setting is customisable and every reset works, and it is why Obsidianite (25 `!important`s, values baked into selectors) could never have had good Style Settings support.

---

## 2. Three-tier token system

### Tier 1 — Primitives (`--prime-c-*`)

Raw colour ramps. **Never referenced outside Tier 2.** Not exposed in Style Settings.

```css
:root {
  /* Neutral ramp — cool violet-tinted, derived from Obsidianite #100e17 / #191621 */
  --prime-c-neutral-0:   hsl(258, 24%,  4%);
  --prime-c-neutral-50:  hsl(258, 24%,  7%);   /* ≈ #0d0b12  background-primary-alt */
  --prime-c-neutral-100: hsl(255, 26%,  7%);   /* ≈ #100e17  background-primary     */
  --prime-c-neutral-200: hsl(255, 19%, 11%);   /* ≈ #191621  background-secondary   */
  --prime-c-neutral-300: hsl(255, 16%, 16%);
  --prime-c-neutral-400: hsl(252, 12%, 28%);
  --prime-c-neutral-500: hsl(250, 10%, 45%);
  --prime-c-neutral-600: hsl(250,  8%, 62%);
  --prime-c-neutral-700: hsl(250,  6%, 75%);   /* ≈ #bebebe  text-normal            */
  --prime-c-neutral-800: hsl(250,  8%, 88%);
  --prime-c-neutral-900: hsl(250, 20%, 97%);

  /* Cyan ramp — Obsidianite's primary accent #0fb6d6 */
  --prime-c-cyan-300: hsl(190, 88%, 72%);
  --prime-c-cyan-400: hsl(190, 92%, 62%);      /* ≈ #3dd7fb */
  --prime-c-cyan-500: hsl(190, 87%, 45%);      /* ≈ #0fb6d6  THE accent             */
  --prime-c-cyan-600: hsl(190, 87%, 36%);
  --prime-c-cyan-700: hsl(190, 84%, 27%);

  /* Magenta ramp — Obsidianite's secondary accent #f4569d */
  --prime-c-magenta-300: hsl(333, 88%, 78%);
  --prime-c-magenta-500: hsl(333, 88%, 65%);   /* ≈ #f4569d */
  --prime-c-magenta-700: hsl(333, 70%, 45%);

  /* Support ramps — blue / purple / green / amber / red */
  --prime-c-blue-500:   hsl(221, 89%, 72%);    /* ≈ #7aa2f7 */
  --prime-c-purple-500: hsl(261, 87%, 78%);    /* ≈ #bb9af7 */
  --prime-c-green-500:  hsl(151, 68%, 58%);
  --prime-c-amber-500:  hsl( 42, 96%, 62%);
  --prime-c-red-500:    hsl(358, 89%, 66%);
}
```

**Design notes**
- All values in **`hsl()`** — required for Style Settings `hsl-split` derivation and for programmatic lightness shifts.
- Obsidianite's exact colours are preserved as the ramp anchors (`-500`), so Prime looks like Obsidianite out of the box.
- The ramp numbering is **light → dark ascending in the light set, and reused unchanged for dark** — i.e. one ramp, two mappings, rather than Primary's two parallel ramps. This halves the token count and makes future colour profiles a matter of swapping ~8 hue anchors.

### Tier 2 — Semantic tokens (`--prime-*`)

The layer users actually customise. Split across the three Style-Settings-compatible scopes.

```css
/* ---- Structure: mode-independent ---- */
body {
  --prime-radius-s: 4px;
  --prime-radius-m: 8px;
  --prime-radius-l: 12px;

  --prime-space-1: 4px;
  --prime-space-2: 8px;
  --prime-space-3: 12px;
  --prime-space-4: 16px;
  --prime-space-6: 24px;
  --prime-space-8: 32px;

  --prime-border-width: 1px;

  --prime-font-text: 'Rubik', var(--font-text-theme), sans-serif;
  --prime-font-interface: var(--prime-font-text);
  --prime-font-monospace: 'JetBrains Mono', 'Cascadia Code', var(--font-monospace-theme), monospace;
  --prime-font-size: 18px;
  --prime-font-weight: 450;

  /* Motion — see 09-* */
  --prime-motion-superfast: 80ms;
  --prime-motion-fast:      160ms;
  --prime-motion-moderate:  260ms;
  --prime-motion-slow:      400ms;
  --prime-motion-scale:     1;              /* global multiplier */
  --prime-ease-standard:  cubic-bezier(0.4, 0.0, 0.2, 1);
  --prime-ease-overshoot: cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1);
  --prime-ease-swing:     cubic-bezier(0.45, 0.05, 0.55, 0.95);
  --prime-ease-exit:      cubic-bezier(0.4, 0.0, 1, 1);
}

/* ---- Dark palette ---- */
.theme-dark {
  --prime-bg-primary:     var(--prime-c-neutral-100);
  --prime-bg-primary-alt: var(--prime-c-neutral-50);
  --prime-bg-secondary:   var(--prime-c-neutral-200);
  --prime-bg-elevated:    var(--prime-c-neutral-300);

  --prime-text-normal: var(--prime-c-neutral-700);
  --prime-text-muted:  var(--prime-c-neutral-600);
  --prime-text-faint:  var(--prime-c-neutral-500);   /* ← FIXED: a real faint grey */
  --prime-text-on-accent: var(--prime-c-neutral-0);

  --prime-accent:        var(--prime-c-cyan-500);
  --prime-accent-hover:  var(--prime-c-cyan-400);
  --prime-accent-muted:  var(--prime-c-cyan-600);
  --prime-accent-alt:    var(--prime-c-magenta-500);
  --prime-accent-alt-hover: var(--prime-c-magenta-300);

  --prime-border:       color-mix(in srgb, var(--prime-accent) 12%, transparent);
  --prime-border-hover: color-mix(in srgb, var(--prime-accent) 40%, transparent);
}

/* ---- Light palette — deliberately designed, NOT inverted (brief §16) ---- */
.theme-light {
  --prime-bg-primary:     hsl(250, 30%, 99%);
  --prime-bg-primary-alt: hsl(250, 24%, 96%);
  --prime-bg-secondary:   hsl(250, 22%, 97%);
  --prime-bg-elevated:    hsl(  0,  0%,100%);

  --prime-text-normal: hsl(255, 22%, 16%);
  --prime-text-muted:  hsl(253, 12%, 40%);
  --prime-text-faint:  hsl(252,  9%, 58%);
  --prime-text-on-accent: hsl(0, 0%, 100%);

  --prime-accent:       var(--prime-c-cyan-600);   /* darker — cyan-500 fails AA on white */
  --prime-accent-hover: var(--prime-c-cyan-700);
  --prime-accent-muted: var(--prime-c-cyan-500);
  --prime-accent-alt:   var(--prime-c-magenta-700);
  --prime-accent-alt-hover: var(--prime-c-magenta-500);

  --prime-border:       color-mix(in srgb, var(--prime-accent) 18%, transparent);
  --prime-border-hover: color-mix(in srgb, var(--prime-accent) 45%, transparent);
}
```

**Note the light-mode accent shift.** `--prime-c-cyan-500` (#0fb6d6) on white gives a contrast ratio of roughly 2.3 : 1 — well below WCAG AA. Light mode therefore uses `cyan-600`. This is the kind of decision the brief means by "do not simply invert colors" (§16).

### Tier 3 — Component tokens

Derived from Tier 2, with inert defaults so every token exists and is settable even when unused (Primary's pattern, P-9).

```css
body {
  /* Headings */
  --prime-h1-size: 34px;  --prime-h1-weight: 600;  --prime-h1-spacing: normal;
  --prime-h2-size: 26px;  --prime-h2-weight: 600;  --prime-h2-spacing: normal;
  --prime-h3-size: 22px;  --prime-h3-weight: 600;
  --prime-h4-size: 19px;  --prime-h4-weight: 600;   /* was 18px — restores hierarchy */
  --prime-h5-size: 17px;  --prime-h5-weight: 600;
  --prime-h6-size: 16px;  --prime-h6-weight: 600;
  --prime-h-underline-width: 1px;
  --prime-h-margin-top: var(--prime-space-8);
  --prime-h-margin-bottom: var(--prime-space-4);

  /* Checkbox */
  --prime-checkbox-size: 17px;
  --prime-checkbox-radius: var(--prime-radius-s);
  --prime-checkbox-border-width: 2px;
  --prime-checkbox-gap: var(--prime-space-2);
  --prime-checkbox-marker-size: 68%;

  /* Inline code */
  --prime-inline-code-radius: var(--prime-radius-s);
  --prime-inline-code-padding: 1px 5px;

  /* Divider */
  --prime-divider-thickness: 1px;
  --prime-divider-margin: 4em;
  --prime-divider-glyph: '§';
  --prime-divider-glyph-rotate: 60deg;
}

.theme-dark, .theme-light {
  --prime-h1-color: var(--prime-accent);
  --prime-h2-color: var(--prime-text-normal);
  /* …h3–h6… */
  --prime-h-underline-color: var(--prime-accent-alt);

  --prime-checkbox-bg:             transparent;
  --prime-checkbox-border:         var(--prime-text-faint);
  --prime-checkbox-checked-bg:     var(--prime-accent);
  --prime-checkbox-checked-border: var(--prime-accent);
  --prime-checkbox-marker:         var(--prime-bg-primary);

  --prime-inline-code-color: var(--prime-accent);
  --prime-inline-code-bg:    color-mix(in srgb, var(--prime-accent) 8%, transparent);

  --prime-divider-color: var(--prime-accent);
  --prime-divider-glyph-color: var(--prime-accent-alt);
}
```

### Tier 4 — Obsidian bridge

**Critical for compatibility.** Prime assigns Obsidian's own variables *from* Tier 2. This is what makes native features and third-party plugins (including Task List Kanban, which reads `--background-primary`, `--interactive-accent`, `--text-muted`, `--radius-s`, `--size-4-2`) look correct without any plugin-specific CSS.

```css
.theme-dark, .theme-light {
  --background-primary:       var(--prime-bg-primary);
  --background-primary-alt:   var(--prime-bg-primary-alt);
  --background-secondary:     var(--prime-bg-secondary);
  --background-secondary-alt: var(--prime-bg-primary-alt);
  --background-modifier-border:        var(--prime-border);
  --background-modifier-border-hover:  var(--prime-border-hover);
  --background-modifier-border-focus:  var(--prime-accent);

  --text-normal: var(--prime-text-normal);
  --text-muted:  var(--prime-text-muted);
  --text-faint:  var(--prime-text-faint);
  --text-accent: var(--prime-accent);
  --text-accent-hover: var(--prime-accent-hover);
  --text-on-accent: var(--prime-text-on-accent);

  --interactive-accent:       var(--prime-accent);
  --interactive-accent-hover: var(--prime-accent-hover);
  --interactive-accent-rgb:   var(--prime-accent-rgb);   /* ← FIXED: real r,g,b triplet */

  --checkbox-size:   var(--prime-checkbox-size);
  --checkbox-radius: var(--prime-checkbox-radius);
  --checkbox-color:  var(--prime-checkbox-checked-bg);
  --checkbox-border-color: var(--prime-checkbox-border);

  --radius-s: var(--prime-radius-s);
  --radius-m: var(--prime-radius-m);
  --radius-l: var(--prime-radius-l);

  --font-text-theme: var(--prime-font-text);
  --font-monospace-theme: var(--prime-font-monospace);
}
```

`--prime-accent-rgb` is produced by Style Settings' `alt-format` on the accent colour setting, emitting `rgb-values` alongside the main value. This resolves Obsidianite defect F-6.

---

## 3. Data flow

```
Tier 1  --prime-c-cyan-500        (primitive; fixed ramp; not user-facing)
   ↓
Tier 2  --prime-accent            (semantic; ← Style Settings writes here)
   ↓
Tier 3  --prime-h1-color          (component; ← Style Settings may also write here)
   ↓
Tier 4  --text-accent             (Obsidian bridge; plugins read here)
   ↓
        .markdown-rendered h1 { color: var(--prime-h1-color); }
```

A user changing one accent colour cascades to headings, links, checkboxes, tags, inline code, borders, and every plugin that reads `--interactive-accent` — while still being able to override any individual component token afterwards.

---

## 4. File / module structure

Obsidian loads exactly **one** `theme.css`. Maintainability requires many files. Resolution: multi-file source + a trivial concatenating build.

```
Obsidian Prime/
├── theme.css                    ← BUILD OUTPUT (the shipped file; do not hand-edit)
├── manifest.json
├── README.md
├── LICENSE
├── package.json                 ← build script only, no runtime deps
├── build.mjs                    ← ~40 lines: read order, concat, banner, minify (optional)
│
├── src/
│   ├── 00-banner.css                 Licence + attribution header (always first)
│   ├── 01-settings.css               The /* @settings */ YAML block
│   │
│   ├── tokens/
│   │   ├── 10-primitives.css         Tier 1 ramps
│   │   ├── 11-semantic-shared.css    Tier 2 — body scope (structure/motion/type)
│   │   ├── 12-semantic-dark.css      Tier 2 — .theme-dark palette
│   │   ├── 13-semantic-light.css     Tier 2 — .theme-light palette
│   │   ├── 14-components.css         Tier 3
│   │   └── 15-obsidian-bridge.css    Tier 4
│   │
│   ├── base/
│   │   ├── 20-reset.css              Scrollbars, selection, focus rings
│   │   └── 21-typography.css         Body text, paragraphs, emphasis
│   │
│   ├── ui/
│   │   ├── 30-workspace.css          Ribbon, sidebars, splits, drop overlay
│   │   ├── 31-titlebar-statusbar.css
│   │   ├── 32-tabs.css
│   │   ├── 33-explorer.css           File explorer, bookmarks, outline, search
